EXAMPLE_LONG_PROMPT_1 = (
    "You are acting in the capacity of a Principal Distributed Systems Architect, Enterprise Infrastructure Strategist, "
    "and Chief Site Reliability Engineer (SRE) leading a multi-year digital transformation initiative for a Tier-1 international "
    "commercial bank. The executive board has issued an uncompromising directive: completely modernize and migrate the institution's "
    "core transaction processing engine—currently operating as a monolithic, synchronous C++/COBOL application hosted on legacy IBM "
    "mainframe infrastructure—into a modern, cloud-native, event-driven microservices architecture. The target architecture must run "
    "on managed Kubernetes (such as AWS EKS or Google Cloud GKE) across three geographic cloud regions in an active-active-active "
    "multi-region deployment topology. The modern system must comfortably support a peak load of at least 150,000 transactions per "
    "second (TPS) while maintaining strict zero-data-loss guarantees, end-to-end data encryption, sub-50ms P99 latencies, and total "
    "regulatory compliance with PCI-DSS 4.0, GDPR, SOC 2 Type II, and DORA (Digital Operational Resilience Act).\n\n"
    "Draft an architectural design document, system evaluation report, and execution strategy blueprint structured into five detailed sections:\n\n"
    "SECTION 1: EXECUTIVE SUMMARY, MIGRATION PARADIGM & BUSINESS JUSTIFICATION\n"
    "   - Detail the primary business drivers motivating this transformation: achieving 99.999% operational uptime (\"five nines\"), "
    "reducing Recovery Time Objective (RTO) under 5 seconds, reducing Recovery Point Objective (RPO) strictly to zero, lowering infrastructure "
    "compute costs, and enabling CI/CD pipelines capable of executing multiple production deployments daily without downtime.\n"
    "   - Perform a comparative evaluation of the Strangler Fig migration pattern versus a traditional \"Big-Bang\" full system rewrite. "
    "Explain how API Gateways (e.g., Kong, Envoy), Change Data Capture (CDC) pipelines leveraging Debezium and Kafka Connect, and Domain-Driven "
    "Design (DDD) bounded contexts will isolate legacy database tables while maintaining real-time dual-write consistency during transition.\n"
    "   - Define explicit Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) to track engineering deployment velocity, fault tolerance, memory/CPU efficiency, "
    "cloud cost optimization, and end-to-end request latencies across every phase of the migration lifecycle.\n\n"
    "SECTION 2: DISTRIBUTED DATA MANAGEMENT, CONSISTENCY MODELS & TRANSACTIONAL INTEGRITY\n"
    "   - Analyze the shift from a centralized relational database with multi-table ACID transactions to a decentralized database-per-service "
    "topology utilizing PostgreSQL, Amazon DynamoDB, Apache Cassandra, and Redis enterprise clusters.\n"
    "   - Compare asynchronous event streaming via Apache Kafka and Apache Pulsar against traditional distributed Two-Phase Commit (2PC) protocols. "
    "Detail why 2PC fails at massive cloud scale due to blocking network locks, network partition vulnerabilities, and throughput bottlenecks.\n"
    "   - Provide a step-by-step breakdown of the Saga Pattern for distributed financial transfers across multiple independent microservices. "
    "Compare Orchestration (using workflow engines like Temporal or AWS Step Functions) with Choreography. Include compensating transaction "
    "protocols for handling partial failures, network timeouts, out-of-order events, and idempotent consumer message deduplication.\n"
    "   - Apply CAP and PACELC theorems to analyze technical trade-offs in enforcing eventual consistency, causal consistency, and strong consistency "
    "across globally replicated, low-latency multi-region read replicas.\n\n"
    "SECTION 3: HIGH AVAILABILITY, SYSTEM RESILIENCY & ADVANCED OBSERVABILITY STACK\n"
    "   - Design a self-healing fault-isolation architecture incorporating adaptive circuit breakers (e.g., Resilience4j), dynamic distributed "
    "rate limiters, token bucket algorithms, client-side load balancing, bulkheading, and exponential backoff with randomized full jitter.\n"
    "   - Specify a zero-trust network security model within a service mesh (e.g., Istio, Linkerd), including mandatory mutual TLS (mTLS) "
    "encryption, fine-grained Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) policies, and cryptographic workload identity attestation via SPIFFE/SPIRE.\n"
    "   - Design a unified observability framework combining open-standard distributed tracing (OpenTelemetry), structured log indexing "
    "(FluentBit, Elasticsearch, Grafana Loki), and high-cardinality metric aggregation (Prometheus, Thanos, Grafana) to isolate P99 latency spikes.\n\n"
    "SECTION 4: PROGRESSIVE DELIVERY, GITOPS & AUTOMATED TRAFFIC ROUTING\n"
    "   - Detail an automated GitOps deployment workflow using ArgoCD or Flux CD to manage declarative Infrastructure-as-Code (Terraform, Pulumi, Helm charts).\n"
    "   - Explain automated Progressive Delivery patterns using Canary deployments and Blue/Green environments. Define automated rollback criteria "
    "triggered by real-time HTTP 5xx error budgets, synthetic transaction failure rates, and P99 latency deviations.\n\n"
    "SECTION 5: COMPREHENSIVE RISK MATRIX & EMERGENCY OPERATIONAL PLAYBOOKS\n"
    "   - Construct a structured matrix detailing top technical risks: data drift during double-write phases, cascading microservice outages, "
    "cloud vendor lock-in, and unexpected cross-region network egress costs.\n"
    "   - Provide step-by-step operational playbooks, failover runbooks, and manual intervention protocols for each identified risk factor."
)

